<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<!--
    Surfaces — the boxes.

    A surface exists only when its content is independently actionable. Grouping alone is a header plus
    spacing, not a box; a border drawn round things that merely belong together adds a line the eye has to
    resolve and buys nothing. That rule is why this file is short and should stay short.

    Elevation, four levels, one shadow:

      L0  the Mica backdrop. Nothing opaque is painted over it, or the backdrop stops being visible and the
          window stops looking like a Windows window.
      L1  grouped content — a settings group, a section of the About page.
      L2  an interactive card: a console tile, a discovered-console row.
      L3  transient over content — a flyout, a dialog, a panel over the video. THE ONLY place ThemeShadow
          appears.

    L2 NEVER gets a drop shadow. Windows 11 separates cards with stroke and fill, and a shadowed card is the
    loudest "this app came from somewhere else" tell there is. If a card is not reading as separate, the fix
    is its fill or its stroke, never a shadow.

    In-app acrylic belongs to L3 and only over content genuinely behind it — over video correct, over Mica
    wrong, because double-blurring goes muddy. When the user has transparency off, every acrylic here must
    fall back to a solid brush; that is decided in code, since a dictionary cannot read the setting.
-->
<ResourceDictionary
    xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

    <!--  L1/L2 content card: the stock card fill and stroke at Ripcord's card radius.  -->
    <Style x:Key="RipcordCardStyle" TargetType="Border">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{ThemeResource CardBackgroundFillColorDefaultBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{ThemeResource CardStrokeColorDefaultBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
        <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{StaticResource RipcordCardCornerRadius}" />
    </Style>

    <!--  A small rounded label for a single fact: version, licence, support level.  -->
    <Style x:Key="RipcordChipStyle" TargetType="Border">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{ThemeResource ControlFillColorDefaultBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{ThemeResource ControlStrokeColorDefaultBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
        <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{StaticResource RipcordPillCornerRadius}" />
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="10,2" />
        <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        A controller button's badge in the hint bar. A style rather than brushes the bar looks up in code: a
        looked-up brush is fixed to the theme it was found in, so after a switch to light the bar would keep
        dark badges and white verbs on a light page. ThemeResource setters follow the theme.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="RipcordHintBadgeStyle" TargetType="Border">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{ThemeResource ControlFillColorSecondaryBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{ThemeResource ControlStrokeColorDefaultBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
        <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{StaticResource RipcordTagCornerRadius}" />
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="8,2" />
        <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
    </Style>

    <!--  A state pill: tighter padding and a dimmer fill than a chip.  -->
    <Style x:Key="RipcordPillStyle" TargetType="Border">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{ThemeResource ControlFillColorSecondaryBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{ThemeResource ControlStrokeColorDefaultBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
        <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{StaticResource RipcordPillCornerRadius}" />
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="8,2" />
        <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
    </Style>

    <!--  The one capability worth the accent fill, so the row has a headline rather than four equal tokens.  -->
    <Style
        x:Key="RipcordAccentPillStyle"
        BasedOn="{StaticResource RipcordPillStyle}"
        TargetType="Border">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{ThemeResource AccentFillColorDefaultBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{ThemeResource AccentFillColorDefaultBrush}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        A card whose content is a row rather than a layout of its own, so the padding belongs to the card. The
        settings page is all of these; the About page's cards arrange their own interiors and take the bare
        card above instead.
    -->
    <Style
        x:Key="RipcordRowCardStyle"
        BasedOn="{StaticResource RipcordCardStyle}"
        TargetType="Border">
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="16,12" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        A hairline between rows of a table. A divider, not a surface: it exists because the rows are a single
        Grid rather than a card each, which is what keeps the labels aligned in one column whatever their
        length — including at the larger text sizes the Display settings offer.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="RipcordRowDividerStyle" TargetType="Border">
        <Setter Property="Height" Value="1" />
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{ThemeResource DividerStrokeColorDefaultBrush}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        One step's dash in a progress indicator: filled once reached, faded by the caller before. Shared rather
        than owned by the pairing flow because "a short run of dashes showing how far through you are" is a
        pattern, not a page — a first-run or account-setup flow wants the identical mark, and a second copy is
        how two indicators end up different heights.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="RipcordStepDashStyle" TargetType="Rectangle">
        <Setter Property="Height" Value="4" />
        <Setter Property="RadiusX" Value="2" />
        <Setter Property="RadiusY" Value="2" />
        <Setter Property="Fill" Value="{ThemeResource AccentFillColorDefaultBrush}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        ================================================================================================
        Focus engagement is off, app-wide

        A Slider defaults to IsFocusEngagementEnabled="True": directional focus will not leave it until the
        control has been explicitly "engaged" and then disengaged, and both are defined purely in terms of
        gamepad key events — GamepadA, GamepadB and friends arriving through KeyDown. In a WinUI 3 DESKTOP app
        those events never arrive: there is no CoreWindow, so this app reads the pad itself and calls the focus
        engine directly. Engagement can therefore never be entered, and never exited.

        The setter is here because the engagement state is unreachable in a desktop app, so leaving it on
        would leave a trap for any gamepad key path. It has not been observed to fix a focus bug, and is not
        the remedy for focus that will not leave a slider: FocusPilot handing the focus search a tooltip as
        its search root has that symptom, and the slider is where it shows, because a slider is where a user
        sits still long enough for a tooltip to appear.
        ================================================================================================
    -->
    <Style TargetType="Slider">
        <Setter Property="IsFocusEngagementEnabled" Value="False" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        A ComboBox defaults to 32 tall, which is a mouse target. Settings is a column of them, and on a
        handheld each is a chance to open the wrong one. Raised to the touch minimum here rather than on
        each instance, so the next combo anyone adds is right without being told.

        Neither this nor the Slider rule above is a surface. They are the app's two implicit control
        defaults, and they sit in this dictionary because it is merged app-wide.
    -->
    <Style TargetType="ComboBox">
        <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="{StaticResource RipcordTouchTarget}" />
    </Style>

</ResourceDictionary>
